{:activity/_scope [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069598} :activity/id #uuid "694f32b9-ef02-56c2-a6ab-8b0b5d3806a7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070021 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines $y = x + 2$ and $y = 2x + 1.$ A sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=x+2$ and $y=2x+1.$ So, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=x+2$ into the second equation $y=2x+1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =2x \\\\ 1 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1, \\, y=3.$ So, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39070934-9f3c-5858-8959-365242cf0563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ca9afc5-3404-515f-b1c6-ea4d5161c29a" :step/math-academy-id 143915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068481 :question/id #uuid "5cf6d36a-0e67-5f16-befd-32d30cf4b930" :question/math-academy-id "e-14010" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1$ sketched below.\n\n![](images/e6/e63b2ac5fe2bdfb905fe3c86706be4a556e5322698f9fd26ee115dba0a886f4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+3 \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+3$ into the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =x-1 \\\\ 5x & =x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1, \\, y=-2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e122a1fe-f340-5af6-9908-68ad52f89731" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116be24f-5fd6-5889-ac0f-f72b509821b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530895} :answer-field/id #uuid "328893f1-1bfe-5d8b-b29a-5733b229fb9a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d86f66-2db2-5d8f-b88c-c725aa3c7f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531007} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1bfb5b-8661-579d-bbb2-a888c6e3cec3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5474d40-cfd8-5d52-b2b0-e62e8f7d76fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-208830" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-1 \\\\ y=-4x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=3x-1$ into the second equation $y=-4x+6$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =-4x+6 \\\\ 3x & =-4x+7 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,1,2\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9d0ac740-cc45-5463-9cc3-cf08249fa047" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066107} {:answer-field/id #uuid "d1d273ab-6204-5c78-bb35-cec133a6c00c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066108}] :question/id #uuid "d99ad6a2-c4bd-5bce-954c-6139455e561a" :question/math-academy-id "q-208840" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y = 3x - 2$ and $y =-5x + 2$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b12903-f2c6-59a8-b9db-2e2d25a6c6ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066719} {:answer/id #uuid "10725934-3aa1-51f1-b8fc-04ef19508154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066720} {:answer/id #uuid "18c09968-bc6d-5847-b3c5-0f9f789f8802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-23)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066721} {:answer/id #uuid "1908bac3-54bf-54f6-b2e3-7555af0e077b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066722} {:answer/id #uuid "63c66bc4-5182-5595-ba98-79d3f9940019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066723}] :answer-field/id #uuid "30de8805-845f-5836-81f7-d831a0d179f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066718}] :question/id #uuid "6191e6b0-7f68-57a7-9caa-b8985f4607d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3319" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y =-10x - 3$ and $y =-8x - 7$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a54877-c197-56e8-954f-97fa34f640d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530869} {:answer/id #uuid "979be2e5-b995-5f79-92bc-1452bf76979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531188} {:answer/id #uuid "a8935a5c-80c0-5239-b637-2aac33da488b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531228} {:answer/id #uuid "d03bf538-f673-50d4-bccb-b6bd89abd7f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531294} {:answer/id #uuid "e605f897-7836-56eb-9e13-386de276895a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3570d971-4147-56ab-ae02-49d2d0cf3575" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2377eeaf-5209-5b0e-a180-97a6667f01fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-12345" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=4x+3 \\\\ y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=4x+3$ into the second equation $y=-x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & =-x-2 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=-x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-2 \\\\  & =-(-1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1$ and $y=-1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e14a637-7109-5806-bcf2-23eddc204a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531030} :answer-field/id #uuid "53808067-0f30-5784-bdf6-5a452e079a99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bceb41-aa44-5fba-9341-e4b1ec60a6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531366} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d8dd92-150d-5fba-846f-54ae59acb479" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8fea67f7-ec4c-5ab9-8c67-4d43507c8b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-215406" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+2 \\\\ y=-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+2$ into the second equation $y=-2x-5$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =-2x-5 \\\\ 5x & =-2x-7 \\\\ 7x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=5x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x+2 \\\\  & =5(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-5+2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1,\\,y=-3.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,-1,-3\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b42dbf5-902f-50e2-aab3-1a9d25bf1aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530917} {:answer/id #uuid "672d8afe-7201-53e1-92cc-5c202dfef3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531094} {:answer/id #uuid "9d020f15-d8db-5bcf-93ec-0ae93232bb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531201} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71990b-5a2b-5323-8b8c-0ebd37de1f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531241} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3d317d-90d0-54fa-9845-224abe2a41f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531318} :answer-field/id #uuid "5385bd89-2e2b-59d2-96ee-f5df31ac0e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbb8ab84-c16d-554a-bf88-2b57aaa4afbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-12341" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+4 \\\\ y=3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=-3x+4$ into the second equation $y=3x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =3x-2 \\\\ -6x & =-6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(1)-2 \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are  $(1,1).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} :step/id #uuid "787639d6-4b54-5b06-ab03-6d845df01b6d" :step/math-academy-id 143916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070034 :tutorial/content "When two lines are given in the *standard form*, you may find it easier to use the *elimination method* to find the point of intersection.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "efdf9704-19aa-5a4a-8ac3-220f267bc227" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9140 :tutorial/title "Lines Given in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "69dbdcc7-0c22-5579-903a-39ea4acc15fb" :step/math-academy-id 143920 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068482 :question/id #uuid "7fe8c6a0-f152-5349-9152-b4c018368dbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14012" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ 2x-y=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n![](images/d2/d2cac87bca62cf4fa8cc6ad3c0bd289de7cb0f8c39e742dd6e7731cb90b0e354.svg)\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{2y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ (x+4x) & =(1+14) \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation $x+2y=1$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =1 \\\\ 3+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-2 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=3, \\, y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a81c1cd-7381-5788-8a51-53cecee5f600" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9b6a7fec-39fc-521b-9ef2-cbe46bfbbbeb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066109} {:answer-field/id #uuid "2e61915a-0e8a-5d4a-8276-1f1310b6fea4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066110}] :question/id #uuid "a1ff5714-9dc6-5ac3-8723-153db69493ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-208847" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $5x - 2y = 3$ and $3x - y = 3$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475d2832-c0a5-5c75-967d-16e511aa4933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531018} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a569e7-b9ff-5249-82a9-9efcf843d4cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066130} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2500fd2-0b2a-5394-bf17-dcf3ac8c7ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531216} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1b4ecb-e59f-573e-9fd5-712b1fdac694" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce310a81-63ea-5437-b6a7-508ae82b0078" :question/math-academy-id "q-215423" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=7$ and $3x+2y=11$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ 3x+2y=11 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=14 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (2x+3x) & =(14+11) \\\\ 5x & =25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=5$ into the equation $x-y=7$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =7 \\\\ 5-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=5,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(5,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840417cc-0d2a-5308-8c33-a5870e7c062e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068279} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fb1728-4018-53b2-beba-7a144d9f6149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068280} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d2f5ac-d13d-5027-88ef-982114238c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068281} {:answer/id #uuid "13e4afb3-4c3f-5a98-999a-b1a97904d584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6, 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068282} {:answer/id #uuid "293c2dcd-5ebf-57ef-92f2-8e78ebc5b040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068283}] :answer-field/id #uuid "365b4447-b4c2-5cb8-bc89-984e19fe0128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068278}] :question/id #uuid "c660fb87-3c62-5e7f-94e2-b1313a2e3af0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75876" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x + y = 14$ and $2x - y = 6$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "708c8c65-9e57-55cf-8e02-bdccfea78cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531112} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2a9c14-2818-5a2f-9495-2a1d80fce7a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e231bc-f852-59af-9063-83603b5b0af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531040} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8710304-e047-5b95-b337-f167d5c09c50" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e71161b-a139-5715-8ffb-5d9680416a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-215907" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=6$  and $2x+3y=2$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y=18 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dfdbf3d-1baf-52a6-9f2e-50f60fe73200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531070} :answer-field/id #uuid "029c7e4a-50de-5170-a29d-9c2812bbfd4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8feaa1-6c6b-5ce2-95a0-abd65447e29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530885} :answer-field/id #uuid "169d52f1-75a4-5be7-9c20-85e9f9e1bbb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f0c0760-2f71-5da7-a09a-417c59aab4e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-215425" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $4x+3y=5$ and $-x-6y=4$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $6y$ term that can cancel with the $-6y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ -x-6y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x+6y=10 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+\\cancel{6y} & =10 \\\\ -x-\\cancel{6y} & =4 \\\\ (8x-x) & =(10+4) \\\\ 7x & =14 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $4x+3y=5$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =5 \\\\ 4(2)+3y & =5 \\\\ 8+3y & =5 \\\\ 3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=2,\\,y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(2,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082e1fb8-38d1-5dd8-8994-bb44202783c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560379} {:answer/id #uuid "239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560447} {:answer/id #uuid "7014a716-ba56-5a31-b1a8-377a659ba2df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560601} {:answer/id #uuid "95309e0e-fd50-5c5e-b095-3c20e548ce32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560670} {:answer/id #uuid "ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560830} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-60172" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 2x-2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 &  \\\\ 2x-2y=3 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 4x-4y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08848abb-4fdd-54a8-a134-d62409d65dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560381} {:answer/id #uuid "146b7b45-9602-56bf-9589-403ec424c06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560407} {:answer/id #uuid "7f322004-2d74-5c3e-ab36-7b710e50faaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560635} {:answer/id #uuid "93ad6313-5531-5f5e-b1e8-0e7aa0bd953c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20276d7-269b-565c-a947-89053ba18c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560407} :answer-field/id #uuid "906a9694-dc14-5b8b-9fcf-596a282ca62d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75873" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $2x+y=12$ and $x-2y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $2y$ term that can cancel with the $-2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ x-2y=6 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y=24 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\cancel{2y} & =24 \\\\ x-\\cancel{2y} & =6 \\\\ (4x+x) & =(24+6) \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the equation $x-2y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =6 \\\\ 6-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=0.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(6,0).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "5fda47d7-4b73-57eb-a3db-30d707f29f26" :step/math-academy-id 143921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068483 :question/id #uuid "cceef15c-a242-5486-a830-f6bdf9f07010" :question/math-academy-id "e-14015" :question/problem "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b, \\, y=-4$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+a \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b, \\, y=-4$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=-3b+a \\\\ -4=b-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =b-1 \\\\ -3 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-3b+a \\\\ -4 & =-3(-3)+a \\\\ -4 & =9+a \\\\ -13 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b154eb5d-a45b-5d92-9e33-dd7875327e6e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "34bdd1f3-2657-59ef-9ebc-0757b26b1e5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066132} {:answer-field/id #uuid "761ba261-2a65-56d5-b920-f1075c4fb6d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066133}] :question/id #uuid "e2c71142-0663-5c43-aece-65acd5a25572" :question/math-academy-id "q-215453" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $3x + y = 8$ and $-2x + y = a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(1, b)$.$a =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, b =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077dadaf-7f3a-5c19-8faa-a7332fc5e8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 2, b =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066310} {:answer/id #uuid "2b422338-c880-59a3-94a4-37b2da8f6093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 1, b = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066311} {:answer/id #uuid "3b50c007-fe3f-5707-81b0-b2d0b765fd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a =-1, b = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066312} {:answer/id #uuid "52dd0367-5fad-5c76-a0d7-33ef45933da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 0, b = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066313} {:answer/id #uuid "7e61737b-11f1-51aa-bb8c-2f85bcd8f905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 2, b = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066314}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5b304006-9ea3-5955-8a74-16334ff326ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066309}] :question/id #uuid "e44b3f1c-3c25-5527-b4e8-00338f468c6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-24481" :question/problem "The lines $y = x + 1$ and $y = 3x - a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(1, b)$. Find the parameters $a$ and $b$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d79f727-28b1-5c7a-a40d-c1d970975032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531172} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ceb5866-3821-5721-8ddd-69dfc4a85ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9a098d-ebc1-504f-8f6e-6368dd1908a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530926} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad9b32b-e694-53a4-b684-a9c3d5526acf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1afc104b-55b0-5a4c-8495-1467f6f4a4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-215456" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $4x+y=a$ and $7x+2y=-2$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(-2,b).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(-2,b)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=-2,\\,y=b$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y=a \\\\ 7x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=-2,\\,y=b$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+b=a \\\\ 7(-2)+2b=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8+b=a \\\\ b=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we know that $b=6.$ Then, we can substitute $b=6$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+b & =a \\\\ -8+6 & =a \\\\ -2 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c84ced-06a7-5128-a357-8450c25a2e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531151} :answer-field/id #uuid "916ed4c7-e602-5f6e-96c2-1df4406aef2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531482} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1758d0a-5deb-56ae-9015-8ac086050fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531272} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8a4f46-631a-5834-bccb-19f61173479f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f0810d4-95d5-5ec1-a80a-fff5b7ccd8fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-215449" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $y=-2x+2$ and $y=-5x-a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b,8).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,8)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b,\\,y=8$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+2 \\\\ y=-5x-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b,\\,y=8$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2(b)+2 \\\\ 8=-5(b)-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2b+2 \\\\ 8=-5b-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $b=-3.$ Then, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =-5b-a \\\\ 8 & =-5(-3)-a \\\\ 8 & =15-a \\\\ -7 & =-a \\\\ 7 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560361} {:answer/id #uuid "156f3b79-c1f4-564c-9343-71d9780571e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560409} {:answer/id #uuid "46ace411-49e7-5267-8ce7-27b0b740cb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560519} {:answer/id #uuid "602e967c-a929-567a-9257-15cb77561af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560571} {:answer/id #uuid "6a59c9dc-7269-58a8-99a5-540b40f347b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=4,\\,b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560361} :answer-field/id #uuid "799a5806-fdb3-56b9-abdb-217c2f17b6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea2f44ab-dddd-5fb6-b2ba-34d59e15c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-60182" :question/problem "The lines $y=3x-b$ and $y=x+5$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(a,2).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(a,2)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=a,\\,y=2$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-b \\\\ y=x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=a,\\,y=2$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=3a-b \\\\ 2=a+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =a+5 \\\\ -3 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3a-b \\\\ 2 & =3(-3)-b \\\\ 2 & =-9-b \\\\ 11 & =-b \\\\ -11 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $b=-11.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Parameters Given a Point of Intersection"} :step/id #uuid "3b997cef-985b-5abc-bc48-d1fbfa6ebd29" :step/math-academy-id 143925}] :activity/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068945} {:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561085} :activity/id #uuid "e8daa4b3-d5d0-5c1b-8d9c-0074d9d5ac3c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082e1fb8-38d1-5dd8-8994-bb44202783c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560379} {:answer/id #uuid "239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560447} {:answer/id #uuid "7014a716-ba56-5a31-b1a8-377a659ba2df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560601} {:answer/id #uuid "95309e0e-fd50-5c5e-b095-3c20e548ce32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560670} {:answer/id #uuid "ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560830} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-60172" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 2x-2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 &  \\\\ 2x-2y=3 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 4x-4y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "a7f1daf6-e6d1-50d5-b72e-f7db11acb306" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560361} {:answer/id #uuid "156f3b79-c1f4-564c-9343-71d9780571e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560409} {:answer/id #uuid "46ace411-49e7-5267-8ce7-27b0b740cb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560519} {:answer/id #uuid "602e967c-a929-567a-9257-15cb77561af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560571} {:answer/id #uuid "6a59c9dc-7269-58a8-99a5-540b40f347b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=4,\\,b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560361} :answer-field/id #uuid "799a5806-fdb3-56b9-abdb-217c2f17b6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea2f44ab-dddd-5fb6-b2ba-34d59e15c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-60182" :question/problem "The lines $y=3x-b$ and $y=x+5$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(a,2).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(a,2)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=a,\\,y=2$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-b \\\\ y=x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=a,\\,y=2$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=3a-b \\\\ 2=a+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =a+5 \\\\ -3 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3a-b \\\\ 2 & =3(-3)-b \\\\ 2 & =-9-b \\\\ 11 & =-b \\\\ -11 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $b=-11.$"} :step/id #uuid "bfcbaaf6-bdac-5d38-9f64-3b4af118cc04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08848abb-4fdd-54a8-a134-d62409d65dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560381} {:answer/id #uuid "146b7b45-9602-56bf-9589-403ec424c06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560407} {:answer/id #uuid "7f322004-2d74-5c3e-ab36-7b710e50faaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560635} {:answer/id #uuid "93ad6313-5531-5f5e-b1e8-0e7aa0bd953c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20276d7-269b-565c-a947-89053ba18c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560407} :answer-field/id #uuid "906a9694-dc14-5b8b-9fcf-596a282ca62d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75873" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $2x+y=12$ and $x-2y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $2y$ term that can cancel with the $-2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ x-2y=6 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y=24 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\cancel{2y} & =24 \\\\ x-\\cancel{2y} & =6 \\\\ (4x+x) & =(24+6) \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the equation $x-2y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =6 \\\\ 6-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=0.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(6,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "cedb93d7-4708-529a-b82a-6f35378c3f1c"}] :activity/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560359}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/id #uuid "19d807d9-e4fc-5eae-b364-d71f790123dd" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068481 :question/id #uuid "5cf6d36a-0e67-5f16-befd-32d30cf4b930" :question/math-academy-id "e-14010" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1$ sketched below.\n\n![](images/e6/e63b2ac5fe2bdfb905fe3c86706be4a556e5322698f9fd26ee115dba0a886f4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+3 \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+3$ into the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =x-1 \\\\ 5x & =x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1, \\, y=-2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e122a1fe-f340-5af6-9908-68ad52f89731" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116be24f-5fd6-5889-ac0f-f72b509821b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530895} :answer-field/id #uuid "328893f1-1bfe-5d8b-b29a-5733b229fb9a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d86f66-2db2-5d8f-b88c-c725aa3c7f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531007} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1bfb5b-8661-579d-bbb2-a888c6e3cec3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5474d40-cfd8-5d52-b2b0-e62e8f7d76fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-208830" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-1 \\\\ y=-4x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=3x-1$ into the second equation $y=-4x+6$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =-4x+6 \\\\ 3x & =-4x+7 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,1,2\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9d0ac740-cc45-5463-9cc3-cf08249fa047" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066107} {:answer-field/id #uuid "d1d273ab-6204-5c78-bb35-cec133a6c00c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066108}] :question/id #uuid "d99ad6a2-c4bd-5bce-954c-6139455e561a" :question/math-academy-id "q-208840" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y = 3x - 2$ and $y =-5x + 2$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b12903-f2c6-59a8-b9db-2e2d25a6c6ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066719} {:answer/id #uuid "10725934-3aa1-51f1-b8fc-04ef19508154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066720} {:answer/id #uuid "18c09968-bc6d-5847-b3c5-0f9f789f8802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-23)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066721} {:answer/id #uuid "1908bac3-54bf-54f6-b2e3-7555af0e077b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066722} {:answer/id #uuid "63c66bc4-5182-5595-ba98-79d3f9940019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066723}] :answer-field/id #uuid "30de8805-845f-5836-81f7-d831a0d179f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066718}] :question/id #uuid "6191e6b0-7f68-57a7-9caa-b8985f4607d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3319" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y =-10x - 3$ and $y =-8x - 7$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a54877-c197-56e8-954f-97fa34f640d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530869} {:answer/id #uuid "979be2e5-b995-5f79-92bc-1452bf76979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531188} {:answer/id #uuid "a8935a5c-80c0-5239-b637-2aac33da488b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531228} {:answer/id #uuid "d03bf538-f673-50d4-bccb-b6bd89abd7f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531294} {:answer/id #uuid "e605f897-7836-56eb-9e13-386de276895a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3570d971-4147-56ab-ae02-49d2d0cf3575" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2377eeaf-5209-5b0e-a180-97a6667f01fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-12345" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=4x+3$ and $y=-x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=4x+3 \\\\ y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=4x+3$ into the second equation $y=-x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & =-x-2 \\\\ 5x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=-x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x-2 \\\\  & =-(-1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1$ and $y=-1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e14a637-7109-5806-bcf2-23eddc204a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531030} :answer-field/id #uuid "53808067-0f30-5784-bdf6-5a452e079a99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bceb41-aa44-5fba-9341-e4b1ec60a6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531366} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d8dd92-150d-5fba-846f-54ae59acb479" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8fea67f7-ec4c-5ab9-8c67-4d43507c8b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-215406" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+2 \\\\ y=-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+2$ into the second equation $y=-2x-5$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =-2x-5 \\\\ 5x & =-2x-7 \\\\ 7x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=5x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x+2 \\\\  & =5(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-5+2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1,\\,y=-3.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,-1,-3\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b42dbf5-902f-50e2-aab3-1a9d25bf1aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530917} {:answer/id #uuid "672d8afe-7201-53e1-92cc-5c202dfef3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531094} {:answer/id #uuid "9d020f15-d8db-5bcf-93ec-0ae93232bb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531201} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71990b-5a2b-5323-8b8c-0ebd37de1f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531241} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3d317d-90d0-54fa-9845-224abe2a41f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531318} :answer-field/id #uuid "5385bd89-2e2b-59d2-96ee-f5df31ac0e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbb8ab84-c16d-554a-bf88-2b57aaa4afbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-12341" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+4 \\\\ y=3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=-3x+4$ into the second equation $y=3x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =3x-2 \\\\ -6x & =-6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(1)-2 \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are  $(1,1).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068482 :question/id #uuid "7fe8c6a0-f152-5349-9152-b4c018368dbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14012" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ 2x-y=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n![](images/d2/d2cac87bca62cf4fa8cc6ad3c0bd289de7cb0f8c39e742dd6e7731cb90b0e354.svg)\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{2y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ (x+4x) & =(1+14) \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation $x+2y=1$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =1 \\\\ 3+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-2 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=3, \\, y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a81c1cd-7381-5788-8a51-53cecee5f600" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9b6a7fec-39fc-521b-9ef2-cbe46bfbbbeb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066109} {:answer-field/id #uuid "2e61915a-0e8a-5d4a-8276-1f1310b6fea4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066110}] :question/id #uuid "a1ff5714-9dc6-5ac3-8723-153db69493ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-208847" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $5x - 2y = 3$ and $3x - y = 3$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475d2832-c0a5-5c75-967d-16e511aa4933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531018} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a569e7-b9ff-5249-82a9-9efcf843d4cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066130} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2500fd2-0b2a-5394-bf17-dcf3ac8c7ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531216} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1b4ecb-e59f-573e-9fd5-712b1fdac694" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce310a81-63ea-5437-b6a7-508ae82b0078" :question/math-academy-id "q-215423" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=7$ and $3x+2y=11$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ 3x+2y=11 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=14 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (2x+3x) & =(14+11) \\\\ 5x & =25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=5$ into the equation $x-y=7$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =7 \\\\ 5-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=5,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(5,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840417cc-0d2a-5308-8c33-a5870e7c062e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068279} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fb1728-4018-53b2-beba-7a144d9f6149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068280} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d2f5ac-d13d-5027-88ef-982114238c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068281} {:answer/id #uuid "13e4afb3-4c3f-5a98-999a-b1a97904d584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6, 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068282} {:answer/id #uuid "293c2dcd-5ebf-57ef-92f2-8e78ebc5b040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068283}] :answer-field/id #uuid "365b4447-b4c2-5cb8-bc89-984e19fe0128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068278}] :question/id #uuid "c660fb87-3c62-5e7f-94e2-b1313a2e3af0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75876" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x + y = 14$ and $2x - y = 6$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "708c8c65-9e57-55cf-8e02-bdccfea78cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531112} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2a9c14-2818-5a2f-9495-2a1d80fce7a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e231bc-f852-59af-9063-83603b5b0af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531040} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8710304-e047-5b95-b337-f167d5c09c50" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e71161b-a139-5715-8ffb-5d9680416a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-215907" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=6$  and $2x+3y=2$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y=18 \\\\ 2x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dfdbf3d-1baf-52a6-9f2e-50f60fe73200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531070} :answer-field/id #uuid "029c7e4a-50de-5170-a29d-9c2812bbfd4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8feaa1-6c6b-5ce2-95a0-abd65447e29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530885} :answer-field/id #uuid "169d52f1-75a4-5be7-9c20-85e9f9e1bbb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f0c0760-2f71-5da7-a09a-417c59aab4e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-215425" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $4x+3y=5$ and $-x-6y=4$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $6y$ term that can cancel with the $-6y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=5 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ -x-6y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x+6y=10 \\\\ -x-6y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+\\cancel{6y} & =10 \\\\ -x-\\cancel{6y} & =4 \\\\ (8x-x) & =(10+4) \\\\ 7x & =14 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $4x+3y=5$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =5 \\\\ 4(2)+3y & =5 \\\\ 8+3y & =5 \\\\ 3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=2,\\,y=-1.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(2,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082e1fb8-38d1-5dd8-8994-bb44202783c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560379} {:answer/id #uuid "239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560447} {:answer/id #uuid "7014a716-ba56-5a31-b1a8-377a659ba2df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560601} {:answer/id #uuid "95309e0e-fd50-5c5e-b095-3c20e548ce32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560670} {:answer/id #uuid "ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560830} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-60172" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 2x-2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 &  \\\\ 2x-2y=3 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 4x-4y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08848abb-4fdd-54a8-a134-d62409d65dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560381} {:answer/id #uuid "146b7b45-9602-56bf-9589-403ec424c06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560407} {:answer/id #uuid "7f322004-2d74-5c3e-ab36-7b710e50faaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560635} {:answer/id #uuid "93ad6313-5531-5f5e-b1e8-0e7aa0bd953c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20276d7-269b-565c-a947-89053ba18c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560407} :answer-field/id #uuid "906a9694-dc14-5b8b-9fcf-596a282ca62d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75873" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $2x+y=12$ and $x-2y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $2y$ term that can cancel with the $-2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ x-2y=6 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y=24 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\cancel{2y} & =24 \\\\ x-\\cancel{2y} & =6 \\\\ (4x+x) & =(24+6) \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the equation $x-2y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =6 \\\\ 6-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=0.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(6,0).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068483 :question/id #uuid "cceef15c-a242-5486-a830-f6bdf9f07010" :question/math-academy-id "e-14015" :question/problem "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b, \\, y=-4$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+a \\\\ y=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b, \\, y=-4$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=-3b+a \\\\ -4=b-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =b-1 \\\\ -3 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-3b+a \\\\ -4 & =-3(-3)+a \\\\ -4 & =9+a \\\\ -13 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b154eb5d-a45b-5d92-9e33-dd7875327e6e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "34bdd1f3-2657-59ef-9ebc-0757b26b1e5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066132} {:answer-field/id #uuid "761ba261-2a65-56d5-b920-f1075c4fb6d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066133}] :question/id #uuid "e2c71142-0663-5c43-aece-65acd5a25572" :question/math-academy-id "q-215453" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $3x + y = 8$ and $-2x + y = a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(1, b)$.$a =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, b =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077dadaf-7f3a-5c19-8faa-a7332fc5e8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 2, b =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066310} {:answer/id #uuid "2b422338-c880-59a3-94a4-37b2da8f6093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 1, b = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066311} {:answer/id #uuid "3b50c007-fe3f-5707-81b0-b2d0b765fd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a =-1, b = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066312} {:answer/id #uuid "52dd0367-5fad-5c76-a0d7-33ef45933da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 0, b = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066313} {:answer/id #uuid "7e61737b-11f1-51aa-bb8c-2f85bcd8f905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = 2, b = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066314}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5b304006-9ea3-5955-8a74-16334ff326ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066309}] :question/id #uuid "e44b3f1c-3c25-5527-b4e8-00338f468c6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-24481" :question/problem "The lines $y = x + 1$ and $y = 3x - a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(1, b)$. Find the parameters $a$ and $b$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d79f727-28b1-5c7a-a40d-c1d970975032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531172} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ceb5866-3821-5721-8ddd-69dfc4a85ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9a098d-ebc1-504f-8f6e-6368dd1908a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530926} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad9b32b-e694-53a4-b684-a9c3d5526acf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1afc104b-55b0-5a4c-8495-1467f6f4a4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-215456" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $4x+y=a$ and $7x+2y=-2$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(-2,b).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(-2,b)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=-2,\\,y=b$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y=a \\\\ 7x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=-2,\\,y=b$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+b=a \\\\ 7(-2)+2b=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8+b=a \\\\ b=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we know that $b=6.$ Then, we can substitute $b=6$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+b & =a \\\\ -8+6 & =a \\\\ -2 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c84ced-06a7-5128-a357-8450c25a2e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531151} :answer-field/id #uuid "916ed4c7-e602-5f6e-96c2-1df4406aef2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531482} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1758d0a-5deb-56ae-9015-8ac086050fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531272} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8a4f46-631a-5834-bccb-19f61173479f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f0810d4-95d5-5ec1-a80a-fff5b7ccd8fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-215449" :question/problem "Find the parameters $a$ and $b$ given that the lines $y=-2x+2$ and $y=-5x-a$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b,8).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,8)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=b,\\,y=8$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+2 \\\\ y=-5x-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=b,\\,y=8$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2(b)+2 \\\\ 8=-5(b)-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=-2b+2 \\\\ 8=-5b-a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $b=-3.$ Then, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =-5b-a \\\\ 8 & =-5(-3)-a \\\\ 8 & =15-a \\\\ -7 & =-a \\\\ 7 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560361} {:answer/id #uuid "156f3b79-c1f4-564c-9343-71d9780571e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560409} {:answer/id #uuid "46ace411-49e7-5267-8ce7-27b0b740cb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560519} {:answer/id #uuid "602e967c-a929-567a-9257-15cb77561af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560571} {:answer/id #uuid "6a59c9dc-7269-58a8-99a5-540b40f347b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=4,\\,b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560361} :answer-field/id #uuid "799a5806-fdb3-56b9-abdb-217c2f17b6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea2f44ab-dddd-5fb6-b2ba-34d59e15c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-60182" :question/problem "The lines $y=3x-b$ and $y=x+5$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(a,2).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(a,2)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=a,\\,y=2$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-b \\\\ y=x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=a,\\,y=2$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=3a-b \\\\ 2=a+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =a+5 \\\\ -3 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3a-b \\\\ 2 & =3(-3)-b \\\\ 2 & =-9-b \\\\ 11 & =-b \\\\ -11 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $b=-11.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Parameters Given a Point of Intersection"}] :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409}] :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"}
